608	//  engine takes accessor-shaped sources `src(mn, k)`; when the source is a
609	//  FLAT row-major gmem region whose rows are K-CONTIGUOUS and 16B-aligned
610	//  (base/ld multiples of 8 bf16 — true of every fwd/dX operand: the bf16 acts
611	//  and the C1/C1-T weight cache), the staging can be issued as 16-byte
612	//  cp.async copies instead of the 2-byte LDG→reg→STS element loop. These two
613	//  POD sources carry exactly the information that decision needs:
614	//    operator()(mn,k)        — the scalar accessor (identical semantics to the
615	//                              lambdas they replace; the S=1 serial path and
616	//                              any non-ring build still stage through it).
617	//    chunk16(mn,kbase,half)  — gmem address of the 16B half-row (8 bf16) that
618	//                              lands at smem offset half·(MN·8)+mn·8 of the
619	//                              canonical Major-K interleave tile.
620	//    row_valid(mn)           — false ⇒ the row is PAD (ragged N-tile): the
621	//                              ring zero-fills smem and must NOT read gmem
622	//                              (mirrors the `nn < Nout ? .. : 0` guard).
623	//  The A-form has no guard (the engine's A rows are always readable — the
624	//  caller guarantees the region, exactly as the current lambdas assume); the
625	//  B-form guards rows beyond `rows_valid`. A trait gates the engine's ring
626	//  branch at COMPILE TIME, so the dW path (lambda sources; transposed-strided
627	//  reads — ring blocker (b), out of scope) keeps the synchronous staging
628	//  unchanged.
